                                    <h2><span class="shaft"><img id="1091-0" src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/icons/patterns/icon_o.png" width=27 height=27 alt="Omnidirectional" title="Omnidirectional" ><img id="1091-1" src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/icons/patterns/icon_c.png" width=27 height=27 alt="Cardioid" title="Cardioid" ><img id="1091-2" src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/icons/patterns/icon_hc.png" width=27 height=27 alt="Hypercardioid" title="Hypercardioid" ></span>MXL LSC-1B</h2>
                    
                    <h2 class="sub">                    Detachable-Capsule Condenser Microphone</h2>
                    <p>The LSC-1 is a handheld vocal condenser microphone with an unusual feature: beneath the removable ball grille, the condenser capsule can be swapped to change the mic&#8217;s polar pattern. The LSC mics use the same small-diaphragm capsules as the 603S/604 pencil mics.</p><p>With the release of the LSC, MXL also introduced a long-awaited hypercardioid capsule, which is also compatible with MXL&#8217;s pencil mics.</p><p>For stage use, the ability to swap Cardioid for Hypercardioid could be useful when more feedback rejection is required, or to isolate a smaller area of the stage. MXL recommends the Omni capsule primarily for studio use.</p><p>A recessed switch on the rear of the mic body enables a high-pass filter: -18dB at 150Hz.</p><p>The mic ships with all three interchangeable capsules (Cardioid, Hypercardioid, Omni), a mic clip, a microfiber cleaning cloth, and an XLR cable, in a plastic carrying case.</p><p>It is available in about 36 different color combinations. Available body colors include forest green, pearl, red, white, black, champagne, and blue; grille colors include gold, silver, white, and black.<p>The <a href="//recordinghacks.com/microphones/MXL/LSC-1">MXL LSC-1B</a> is also known as: LSC-1N.</p><p>The mic was released in 2010.</p>
